virglrenderer: show Vulkan windows on macOS OpenGL instead of ending the compositor's context

What: when a compositor imports a dma-buf (PIPE_RESOURCE_SET_TYPE) and there is
no EGL (OmacVM: native OpenGL through CGL), the resource gets a plain GL texture
instead of failing. When the memory is a Metal heap (a Venus image: a Vulkan
app's window), the texture is filled from the heap each time a draw samples
it, at most once per command buffer: a Metal blit into a shared buffer when the
heap is private, then glTexSubImage2D. Any other memory leaves the texture
blank. Both cases log once. The GL state the copy touches (texture binding,
unpack buffer, unpack row length/alignment/skips) is put back.
Why: the old non-EGL path needs GL_EXT_memory_object_fd, which macOS OpenGL
does not have, so it returned EINVAL. A decode error ends the whole context:
a Vulkan app (vkcube, vkmark) drawing in a window on Omarchy took Hyprland's
GPU context with it, black desktop until the session restarted. Any Wayland
client could do this with a dma-buf. 3.0.0 hid it with MESA_VK_WSI_DEBUG=sw
in the guest (software WSI), which apps can clear.
Checks (the guest is untrusted): the heap must be a placement heap; the plane's
offset + stride * (height - 1) + width * bytes per pixel must lie inside the
heap; one plane only; the stride must be at least the row and a whole number
of pixels; only uncompressed 2D formats. The texture itself goes through the
normal resource checks and the GPU memory budget, and so does the shared copy
buffer of a private heap (else the window stays blank).
Limits: a copy per compositor frame that samples the window (vkmark full
screen on an M4 Max, median of 3: 678 against 65 with the software WSI). The copy reads
what Venus finished; the guest's present wait orders it, the host does not
add a fence.
Upstream: not sent (upstream's Metal path needs EGL on ANGLE).
Test: Tests/virgl/test-set-type-no-egl.c (build time); VM: vkcube and vkmark
windowed and full screen with Mesa's normal WSI (gpu-robust.md, "Black desktop
root cause").

